//! tr kodlu 2 dil=en
// 21 — CSV, environment variables and time: taking data from the outside world.
// What it teaches: reading a table with parse_csv and read_csv, taking a setting from the environment with env,
// measuring time with time_ms.
// Builds on: files and JSON (19), paths (20), lists (09), none and ?? (03).
// Run (from the project root): tr examples/en/21_csv_and_environment.tr
// The CSV file is written to a temporary folder (deleted when the program ends).

// parse_csv turns CSV text into lists of rows (it does not read a file). A cell in strict number form becomes a
// number; a cell like "007" with a leading zero stays a String.
A030 rows = G603("name,age,code\nAli,30,007\nAyse,25,008")
G100(rows[0], rows[1])
G100(rows[1][1] + 1, G201(rows[1][1]), G201(rows[1][2]))

// read_csv does the same for a file.
A030 file = G120(G126(), "table.csv")
G111(file, "product,count\napple,3\nbanana,5")
A030 lines = G602(file)
G100("number of rows:", lines.Y001)
A010 line A011 lines {
    G100(line)
}
// The count in the header row is a String, in data rows an Int: we sum only the numbers.
G100("total count:", G400([line[1] A010 line A011 lines A001 G201(line[1]) == A200]))

// env gives none if the variable is not defined; the default is written with ??.
G100(G800("TR_EXAMPLE_MISSING_VARIABLE") ?? "default value")

// time_ms is the milliseconds since 1970; the difference of two measurements is a duration.
A030 start = G502()
G500(0.05)
G100("at least 50 ms passed:", G502() - start >= 50)

// --- Try it yourself ---
// 1. Parse your own CSV text and compute the mean of the age column.
// 2. Measure how many ms a loop takes with time_ms.
